---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Seal from "../../components/Seal.astro";
import { base } from "../../lib/base";

const awards = [
  "Invited by the Iranian Government as one of nine Qur'anic scholars from around the world for the International Qur'anic Exhibition, 2007.",
  "Invited to the Third International Conference on Islamic Media, jointly hosted by the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia and the Government of Indonesia, Jakarta, 2013.",
  "Overseas guest of Australia's Multicultural Festival Society, delivering lectures including “New World Order in the Light of Islamic Principles” and “Islamic Model for Control of AIDS.”",
  "Lectures on Islam and Science, and Family Welfare in Islam, at Aligarh Muslim University's Faculty of Islamic Studies and Faculty of Sunni Theology.",
  "Delivered “The Devil of Economic Fundamentalism” to the Economics Society, Aligarh Muslim University.",
  "Aditya Samman, awarded by the Government of Uttar Pradesh at the Saharanpur Mahotsava.",
  "Declared Man of the Year 2014 by Muslim Mirror; profiled again as Man of the Year 2016.",
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Dr. Javed Jamil — physician, Islamic scholar, and originator of Applied Islamics. Biography, theories, and activism."
>
  <article class="about">
    <header class="about__header">
      <div class="eyebrow">Biography</div>
      <h1 class="about__title">Dr. Javed Jamil</h1>
      <p class="about__standfirst">
        Dr. Javed Jamil may well be regarded as one of the most powerful thinkers of the current times in the Islamic
        world. He has launched an ideological onslaught against the scientific and social philosophies of the New
        World Order and their impact on humanity, presenting alternative Islamic paradigms in their place — while
        preparing plans for the comprehensive empowerment of Muslims. His concern is for the whole of humanity: he
        presents Islam not just for Muslims but for the benefit of all mankind. His works span medicine, religion,
        economics, current affairs, physics, political science, philosophy, and Urdu poetry.
      </p>
      <div class="about__links">
        <a href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1aeQryZl7BmlsRe2Fembf5mhnO7RKhoUl/view" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Download biography (ePUB)</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
        <a href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1slkDIl9m-nG0c4gbjbfUQwrE0FazoUkh/view" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Download biography (PDF)</a>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="about__body">
      <p>
        When President Obama, after his election, addressed the Muslim world, an organisation gathered the views of
        one hundred prominent Muslims worldwide. Dr. Jamil was the only Muslim analyst from the Indian subcontinent
        invited to respond. He wrote:
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        “America can avenge the killing of 3,000 Americans by killing two million Muslims. Tell me how Muslims can
        and should avenge the killing of two million Muslim innocents by Americans? If America cannot tolerate the
        extremist Muslims, why should Muslims tolerate the extremist America?”
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        He was also the first Indian Islamic scholar invited by the Iranian government to its annual Qur'anic
        Exhibition, and the only Indian scholar at the Third International Conference on Islamic Media in Jakarta. In
        1994 he became the first Indian Muslim scholar to publish in support of family planning from an Islamic
        viewpoint — with important qualifications — in a book that traditional and Islamic scholars alike described
        as balanced and methodologically sound.
      </p>

      <h2>A Man of Theories</h2>
      <p>
        Dr. Jamil is, above all, a man of theories — propounding unconventional positions across fields, almost all
        of them rooted in Islamic principle but argued in both secular and Islamic frameworks.
      </p>

      <h3>Economic Fundamentalism</h3>
      <p>
        Dr. Jamil has waged what he calls a ruthless war of attrition against <strong>Economic Fundamentalism</strong>
        — the ideology that gives predominance to economics over every other sphere of life, indifferent to its
        effects on health, family peace, and social order. In his account, businessmen were once "cranes" waiting
        for prey to come close; since the Industrial Revolution they have organised into "leopards" that roar and
        rule — mastering the state, remaking law in their interest, and industrialising sex as the commodity that
        "always lays golden eggs."
      </p>

      <h3>Peace Economics &amp; the Theory of Peace</h3>
      <p>
        Against Economic Fundamentalism, Dr. Jamil proposes <strong>Peace Economics</strong>: development that never
        undermines <strong>Grand Peace</strong> — health of the individual, family peace, and social order taken
        together. He rejects narrower definitions of peace, whether the political (absence of conflict), the mystic
        (tranquillity of the soul), or the sociological (distribution of wealth), for a comprehensive one. His
        alternative to the New World Order is the <strong>Natural World Order</strong>: a system built on equal
        weight given to Fundamental Rights, Fundamental Duties, and — the one the corporate world abhors most —
        Fundamental Prohibitions.
      </p>

      <h3>The Dynamic Paradigm of Health</h3>
      <p>
        Dr. Jamil argues that international health policy is itself governed by economic fundamentalism, not health.
        "The worship of Hygeia is not tolerable for Mammon," he writes — health demands discipline and restriction,
        and restriction is a word absent from the lexicon of the bazaar. His Dynamic Paradigm calls for health,
        not profit, to set the terms for smoking, drinking, gambling, and commercial sex alike, and for family
        health to be treated as a matter of civilisational, not merely individual, concern.
      </p>

      <h3>The Universal Theory of Relativity</h3>
      <p>
        Dr. Jamil has also turned his attention to physics, arguing that General Relativity and Big Bang cosmology
        describe "a passive, clumsily stark looking collection of individual groups of matter" that has "lost its
        virility" — an account of the universe with hardly any dynamic existence left in it. His proposed Universal
        Theory of Relativity is an attempt to restore a governing order to that picture.
      </p>

      <h2>Applied Islamics</h2>
      <p>
        Dr. Jamil's unshakeable conviction that the Qur'an is the Book of God led him to found <strong>Applied
        Islamics</strong> as a discipline in its own right — not the study of Islam through the lens of modern
        developments, but the reverse: modern developments in social and scientific fields examined in the light
        of Islamic principle, with alternative Islamic models proposed in their place. He holds that Islam is for
        the whole world, not Muslims alone, and that Muslims must lead the fight against the evils pervading
        society. His life's ambition is an international Institute of Applied Islamics, dedicated to exactly this
        work.
      </p>

      <h2>Social Activist</h2>
      <p>
        Dr. Jamil founded PEACE, an NGO that has run national and district-level seminars on education, health, and
        communal harmony, and has worked with the Government of India and SIFPSA/USAID on health and family welfare
        projects. At 24, he organised the first major demonstration in Saharanpur against the Supreme Court's
        verdict in the Shah Bano case — before the campaign it seeded became one of the most significant Muslim
        mobilisations in independent India's history. In 1985 he launched the "Dakhla Lo" (Take Admission) campaign,
        visiting more than 250 villages across Saharanpur district to lecture on health and education, and later
        organised relief camps during communal violence in the region.
      </p>

      <h2>The Poet</h2>
      <p>
        Dr. Jamil has composed several hundred Urdu ghazals. His first collection, <em>Rahguzar</em>, was published
        by the Fakhruddin Memorial Committee, an organisation of the Government of Uttar Pradesh; a second collection
        followed. He remains lesser-known as a poet than as a thinker, largely by choice — he has generally declined
        invitations to mushairas in favour of other commitments. His poetry carries the same charge as his prose: as
        often aggressive as it is, at times, unguardedly romantic. A selection appears in the <a href={`${base}shayari/`}>Shayari</a> section
        of this site.
      </p>

      <h2>Awards, Positions &amp; Lectures</h2>
      <ul class="about__awards">
        {awards.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
      </ul>

      <p class="about__more-links">
        The full list of Dr. Jamil's published books is in the <a href={`${base}books/`}>Works library</a>; his essays
        and series are collected under <a href={`${base}ideas/`}>Ideas</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <footer class="about__seal">
      <Seal variant="geometric" mode="light" size={40} animated={false} />
    </footer>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about {
    max-width: var(--reading-max);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 72px var(--section-pad-x) 60px;
  }

  .about__header {
    margin-bottom: 44px;
  }

  .about__title {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: clamp(34px, 5.5vw, 58px);
    line-height: 1.02;
    margin: 16px 0 24px;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .about__standfirst {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: 19px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: #33384a;
    margin: 0 0 24px;
  }

  .about__links {
    font-size: 13.5px;
  }

  .about__links a {
    color: var(--accent);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .about__links a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  .about__links span {
    margin: 0 10px;
    color: var(--light-text-faint);
  }

  .about__body {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: 18px;
    line-height: 1.72;
    color: #26262b;
  }

  .about__body p {
    margin: 0 0 22px;
  }

  .about__body h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: clamp(24px, 3.2vw, 32px);
    line-height: 1.15;
    margin: 48px 0 20px;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .about__body h3 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 21px;
    margin: 34px 0 14px;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .about__body blockquote {
    margin: 28px 0;
    padding-left: 26px;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 23px;
    line-height: 1.4;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .about__body a {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .about__awards {
    padding-left: 22px;
    margin: 0 0 22px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 12px;
  }

  .about__awards li {
    font-size: 16.5px;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .about__more-links {
    font-size: 15px;
    color: var(--light-text-muted);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--light-hairline);
    padding-top: 24px;
  }

  .about__seal {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    margin-top: 40px;
  }
</style>
